From de9d886b15cf7fbf132fdea287818b39a566f938 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri John Ledkov <dimitri.ledkov@chainguard.dev>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:22:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Build the legacy provider as a small freestanding module

Reduce the legacy provider to a fixed, minimal algorithm set and make it
linkable without libcrypto, so that the resulting legacy.so depends only
on the C library and can be loaded by a libcrypto it was not built
against.  This is the shape the FIPS provider already has, achieved the
same way: the implementations the module needs are compiled a second time
into liblegacy.a.

The algorithms registered are MD4, MD5, SHA1 and SHA2-224 for digests, and
AES-128-ECB and AES-256-ECB for ciphers, plus DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC
behind a separate switch (see below).  Everything else is dropped:
RC4 and RC4-40 along with the other legacy ciphers and digests, and the
KDF and key-management operations, which existed only to serve
algorithms that are no longer offered and which pulled in EVP fetch and
ASN.1 support.

MD5, SHA1, SHA2-224 and AES were previously reachable only through libcrypto's
exported symbols, so crypto/sha, crypto/aes and crypto/modes now feed
liblegacy.a as crypto/md5 and crypto/md4 already did.  crypto/md4's and
crypto/des's conditions are relaxed accordingly: the module needs its own
copy whether or not deprecated symbols were configured out, and the TDES
EDE3 sources join the single-DES ones there.  A short list of utility
sources in crypto/build.info covers what those implementations call, and
the AES and cipher-common source lists are narrowed so that GCM, CCM, XTS
and AES-CBC-HMAC-SHA are left out.  ciphercommon.c and digestcommon.c
already reach liblegacy.a through the per-provider goals introduced for
the FIPS indicators.

Everything in liblegacy.a is compiled with LEGACY_MODULE defined, and the
code paths that would otherwise pull in most of the library are compiled
out or redirected under it:

  - OSSL_PARAM_get_BN() and OSSL_PARAM_set_BN() are the only users of
    crypto/bn in this module.  Removing them removes BIGNUM support,
    which none of the algorithms above uses.

  - ossl_cipher_tlsunpadblock() and its caller reach tls_pad.c, which
    calls RAND_bytes_ex() on its constant-time failure path.  No
    TLS-capable cipher is registered, so the path is unreachable.

  - The OSSL_CIPHER_PARAM_RANDOM_KEY getter behind
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_rand_key() called RAND_priv_bytes_ex() and would have
    pulled the DRBG into the module.  Both DES key generators now call
    ossl_cipher_generic_rand_bytes(), which uses RAND_priv_bytes_ex()
    normally and, under LEGACY_MODULE, asks the core for entropy through
    the OSSL_FUNC_get_user_entropy / OSSL_FUNC_get_entropy upcalls -- the
    same path the FIPS module uses to seed its DRBGs.  legacyprov.c
    records those callbacks at init with ossl_prov_seeding_from_dispatch(),
    provider_seeding.c is added to liblegacy.a, and PROV_CIPHER_CTX
    carries the provider context so the call can be made.  Encryption and
    decryption never reach this path.

legacyprov.c no longer creates a child OSSL_LIB_CTX either.  All the
algorithms are self-contained and never fetch from the core, and dropping
it removes the last libcrypto dependency.  For the same reason the
OPENSSL_init_crypto(OPENSSL_INIT_NO_ATEXIT) call that 4.0.3 added to the
non-static legacy provider is not carried: there is no libcrypto in this
module to register an atexit handler.

The provider reports its name (OSSL_PROV_PARAM_NAME, shown by "openssl
list -providers") as "Chainguard Legacy Allowed FIPS 140-3 I.G. 2.4.A
Provider".  It is still loaded and configured as "legacy".

Two provider parameters, both defaulting to 0, control what is offered.
They are read through the core's get_params upcall in the query callback
with ossl_prov_ctx_get_bool_param():

  - "enable-legacy-allowed" gates the whole module.  While it is off, the
    query callback answers every operation with an empty algorithm list,
    so nothing from this module can be fetched -- the same outcome as
    "activate = 0" -- but the provider is still loaded, activated and
    registered with the core: OSSL_PROVIDER_available() reports it,
    "openssl list -providers" shows it as active, and its parameters can
    be queried.

  - "enable-des" additionally registers DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC.  The DES
    entries sit at the front of the cipher table so the disabled view is
    the same array starting two entries in.  It has no effect unless
    "enable-legacy-allowed" is on, and a no-des build ignores it.

Either is set with "<name> = 1" in the provider's configuration section,
OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(), or -provparam legacy:<name>=1 on the
command line.  Because the core remembers which operations it has already
asked a provider about, both must be set before the first fetch; this is
noted in the source and README.

Property strings are split by an ALG_NOFIPS() macro.  The digests (MD4,
MD5, SHA1, SHA2-224) and AES-128-ECB / AES-256-ECB are advertised with
"provider=legacy,fips=yes", so that they remain fetchable when an
application sets default properties of "fips=yes".  None of them is
FIPS-approved; each is non-approved but allowed for a specific,
non-security-relevant purpose in the sense of FIPS 140-3 IG 2.4.A --
parsing or verifying data in a format that mandates it, rather than
providing confidentiality, authentication or integrity.  MD5 is required
by the PDF format, SHA1 verifies artifacts in ecosystems that publish SHA1
checksums, SHA2-224 is required by pip as the digest used for its cache
files, MD4 is required by webpack's content hashing, and AES-ECB
derives the masking value for QUIC header protection (RFC 9001) and DTLS
1.3 record number encryption (RFC 9147).  DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are
"provider=legacy,fips=no": an application running with fips=yes default
properties never receives them from this module, and a
fips=yes fetch of DES-EDE3-CBC can only resolve to the FIPS provider's
decrypt-only implementation.

The test configurations that activate the legacy provider --
test/legacy.cnf, test/default-and-legacy.cnf and the 90-test_includes
data file -- set "enable-legacy-allowed", so the test suite continues to
see the full algorithm set.  Those with "activate = false" are unchanged.

README-LEGACY-FREESTANDING.md documents the recommended configure
settings (no-asm and no-secure-memory are needed, no-quic is optional)
and why each is required, the no-des / no-md4 / no-md5 options
that shrink the module further, the build and test-harness prerequisites,
both provider parameters and the first-fetch caveat, the per-algorithm
rationale for the fips=yes / fips=no split and its limits, and
verification and known-answer commands.

Note that "make test" does not work: it builds every test program, and
test/endecode_test and test/evp_extra_test link liblegacy.a alongside
libcrypto.a and hit multiple-definition errors on the duplicated
sources.  Use "make run_tests TESTS=..." as described in the README.
Recipes covering the removed algorithms fail by design, and evp_test
stops a data file at the first cipher it cannot fetch, so the stock DES
data files cannot exercise this module.

Verified on top of openssl-4.0.3 with a no-asm no-quic no-secure-memory
build: legacy.so depends only on libc and exports only
OSSL_provider_init; "openssl list -providers" reports the new name;
"openssl list" shows no legacy algorithms by default,
six with enable-legacy-allowed, eight with enable-des as well, and none
with enable-des alone; RC4 is not fetchable from it; an MD5 fetch with
-propquery provider=legacy succeeds with the switch and fails without it;
MD4, MD5, SHA1 and SHA2-224 match their "abc" known answers when forced
through this provider;
and evp_test on evpmd_md.txt, evpmd_sha.txt and evpciph_aes_common.txt
passes under test/default-and-legacy.cnf.

