+Freestanding legacy provider
+============================
+
+This tree can build `legacy.so` as a *freestanding* provider module: one that
+does not link against `libcrypto`, in the same way the FIPS provider does not.
+The resulting module depends only on the C library, exports a single symbol
+(`OSSL_provider_init`) and can therefore be loaded by a `libcrypto` it was not
+built against.
+
+The algorithm set is deliberately small, and none of it is offered until
+the provider is told to (see "Enabling the provider" below):
+
+| Operation | Algorithms                                                  |
+|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
+| Digest    | MD4, MD5, SHA1, SHA2-224                                    |
+| Cipher    | AES-128-ECB, AES-256-ECB                                    |
+| Cipher, only with `enable-des = 1` | DES-CBC, DES-EDE3-CBC              |
+
+No KDFs, no key management and no other ciphers or digests are registered.
+In particular RC4 and RC4-40 are not offered.
+
+### Enabling the provider
+
+Loading and activating the module registers nothing by itself.  A second
+switch, the provider parameter `enable-legacy-allowed`, defaults to `0`, and
+while it is off every operation query from the core is answered with an empty
+list.  For fetches this is indistinguishable from `activate = 0`; the
+difference is that the provider is loaded, activated and stays registered
+with the core, so `OSSL_PROVIDER_available()` reports it, `openssl list
+-providers` shows it, and its parameters can be queried.  Set it in the
+provider's configuration section:
+
+    [legacy_sect]
+    activate = 1
+    enable-legacy-allowed = 1
+
+or, for the command-line tools, after loading the provider:
+
+    openssl dgst -provider legacy -provparam legacy:enable-legacy-allowed=1 ...
+
+Programmatically, call `OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(prov,
+"enable-legacy-allowed", "1")` on the loaded provider before the first fetch.
+The value is parsed like `enable-des` below, and has to be set before the
+first fetch for the same reason.  `test/legacy.cnf`,
+`test/default-and-legacy.cnf` and the `90-test_includes` data file that
+activate this provider all set it, so the test suite sees the full set.
+
+### Enabling DES
+
+DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are compiled in but not registered unless the
+provider is told to.  The switch is a provider parameter, `enable-des`,
+which defaults to `0`.  Set it in the provider's configuration section:
+
+    [legacy_sect]
+    activate = 1
+    enable-des = 1
+
+or, for the command-line tools, after loading the provider:
+
+    openssl enc -provider legacy -provparam legacy:enable-des=1 ...
+
+Programmatically, call `OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(prov, "enable-des",
+"1")` on the loaded provider before the first fetch.  The value is read
+through the core's `get_params` each time the cipher list is queried, using
+the same parser as the other providers: `1`, `yes`, `true` and `on` enable,
+`0`, `no`, `false` and `off` disable, anything else is treated as the default.
+The core remembers which operations it has already asked a provider about,
+so a value set after the first fetch of that operation is not seen until the
+method store is flushed (for example by activating another provider).
+With `enable-legacy-allowed = 1` alone, `openssl list` shows six algorithms
+from this provider; with `enable-des = 1` as well, eight.  `enable-des` has no
+effect while `enable-legacy-allowed` is off.  A `no-des` build has nothing to
+enable and ignores the parameter.
+
+### Configure options that shrink the set further
+
+The table above is what a default `./Configure` produces.  The usual
+per-algorithm `no-*` options still apply to this module, and remove the
+corresponding entries from it:
+
+| Configure option | Removed from the legacy module |
+|------------------|--------------------------------|
+| `no-des`         | DES-CBC, DES-EDE3-CBC          |
+| `no-md4`         | MD4                            |
+| `no-md5`         | MD5                            |
+
+`no-des` also drops DES-EDE3-CBC from the default and FIPS providers, so
+choose it with the whole build in mind, not just this module.  SHA1, SHA2-224 and
+AES-ECB cannot be configured out.
+
+This branch targets the `openssl-4.0.3` release.  Two build.info details
+differ from the master branch, where this work originated: 4.0.3 has no
+`crypto/aes/aes_cbc_vaes_intrinsic.c`, and it keeps the AES CFB hardware
+backend in its own `cipher_aes_cfb_hw.c` rather than folded into
+`cipher_aes_hw.c`.  Both are accounted for in the source lists here.
+
+FIPS properties
+---------------
+
+The digests (MD4, MD5, SHA1, SHA2-224) and AES-128-ECB / AES-256-ECB are advertised
+with `fips=yes` in addition to `provider=legacy`, so that they remain
+fetchable when an application sets default properties of `fips=yes`:
+
+    [algorithm_sect]
+    default_properties = fips=yes
+
+The ciphers DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are advertised with
+`fips=no` instead.  An application in that configuration therefore never
+gets them from this module (DES not even with `enable-des = 1`), and a
+`fips=yes` fetch of DES-EDE3-CBC can only resolve to the FIPS provider's
+decrypt-only implementation, never to this copy.  To use either,
+fetch with `provider=legacy` or without `fips=yes`.
+
+None of the `fips=yes` algorithms is FIPS-approved.  They are marked this way because
+each is *non-approved, but allowed* for a specific, non-security-relevant
+purpose, in the sense of FIPS 140-3 Implementation Guidance 2.4.A
+("Definition and Requirements of an Approved Mode of Operation").  In each
+case the algorithm is used to parse, decode or de-obfuscate data in a format
+that mandates it — not to provide confidentiality, authentication or
+integrity.
+
+| Algorithm | Why it is required |
+|-----------|--------------------|
+| MD5       | Required by the PDF format itself, including the standard security handler's encryption key derivation and document identifiers. |
+| SHA1      | Widely required by file formats and package ecosystems.  For example, Maven Central publishes `.sha1` checksums, so fetching and verifying JARs requires SHA1. |
+| SHA2-224  | Required by Python's `pip`, which uses SHA-224 as the digest for its cache files, deriving each cache entry's name from the SHA-224 of its key. |
+| MD4       | Required by Node.js `webpack`, whose content hashing has historically defaulted to MD4, used to build deterministic module and chunk lookup tables for its build cache. |
+| AES-ECB   | Header protection in QUIC (RFC 9001, section 5.4.3) and record number encryption in DTLS 1.3 (RFC 9147, section 4.2.3).  Both apply raw AES-ECB to a sample of the packet to derive a mask; the confidentiality of the payload comes from the AEAD, not from this step. |
+
+The `fips=no` ciphers make no claim under IG 2.4.A.  They exist for this
+decode purpose:
+
+| Algorithm | Purpose |
+|-----------|---------|
+| DES-CBC, DES-EDE3-CBC | Decrypting data in legacy formats that fix the cipher: PEM private keys with a `DEK-Info: DES-CBC` or `DES-EDE3-CBC` header, PKCS#8 and PKCS#12 objects protected with `pbeWithMD5AndDES-CBC` or `pbeWithSHA1And3-KeyTripleDES-CBC`, and other archives written by older tooling.  Only CBC mode is offered; ECB, CFB, OFB and DESX are not. |
+
+Two caveats worth being explicit about:
+
+* Marking a service `fips=yes` does not make it approved, and this module
+  implements no approved-security-service indicator (FIPS 140-3 IG 2.4.C).  A
+  caller therefore cannot distinguish, at run time, an approved service from
+  one of these allowed-but-non-approved ones.  An application in an approved
+  mode that fetches `SHA1` or `MD5` will simply get it.
+* Whether these services may appear in an approved mode of a *validated*
+  module is a determination for the validation itself, made against the
+  module's security policy and its defined use of each algorithm.  Marking
+  the property here does not make that determination.
+
+Building
+--------
+
+    ./Configure no-asm no-quic no-secure-memory
+    make -j4 build_generated
+    make -j4 providers/legacy.so
+
+### Recommended configure settings
+
+`no-asm`
+: Required in practice.  With assembly enabled, `cipher_aes_hw_aesni.c`
+  provides one hardware dispatch table covering *every* AES mode, so linking
+  AES-ECB also drags in GCM, CCM, XTS and AES-CBC-HMAC-SHA — roughly 800 KB of
+  unreachable code, and a dependency on the DRBG through GCM's IV generation.
+  Note the trade-off: AES then uses the table-based C implementation in
+  `crypto/aes/aes_core.c`, which is not hardened against cache-timing
+  observation.  Do not use this configuration where that matters.
+
+`no-secure-memory`
+: Required.  `crypto/array_alloc.c` calls `CRYPTO_secure_malloc()`, which pulls
+  in `crypto/mem_sec.c`.  That file is the only user of the `CRYPTO_THREAD`
+  locks and of `OPENSSL_die()`, and satisfying the locks means linking
+  `crypto/threads_pthread.c`, whose RCU implementation reaches into
+  `crypto/context.c` and from there into the entire library context registry —
+  DSO loading, CONF, OBJ, the property store, encoder/decoder caches and more.
+  Disabling secure memory cuts that chain at the root.
+
+`no-quic`
+: Optional, saves one file.  `crypto/packet.c` guards its variable-length
+  integer helpers on `OPENSSL_NO_QUIC`, so this removes the need for
+  `crypto/quic_vlint.c`.
+
+`no-tls` has no useful effect here: it concerns protocol versions in `libssl`,
+and the only `libcrypto`-side code it might reach is `tls_pad.c`, which this
+build already excludes (see below).
+
+### Build gotchas
+
+* Run `make build_generated` first.  `include/openssl/opensslv.h` is generated,
+  and building the `providers/legacy.so` target on its own does not create it.
+  Because `-Iinclude` then finds nothing, the compiler silently falls through to
+  any `openssl/opensslv.h` installed under `/usr/include`, and the version
+  mismatch fails as:
+
+      include/openssl/macros.h:155:2: error: #error "OPENSSL_API_COMPAT expresses an impossible API compatibility level"
+
+* `ar` updates archives in place, so members from an earlier configuration are
+  not removed by a rebuild.  After changing which sources go into
+  `liblegacy.a`, delete it first:
+
+      rm -f providers/liblegacy.a
+
+Verifying
+---------
+
+The module should depend on nothing but the C library:
+
+    $ readelf -d providers/legacy.so | grep NEEDED
+     0x0000000000000001 (NEEDED)             Shared library: [libc.so.6]
+
+    $ nm -D --defined-only providers/legacy.so
+    0000000000006f10 T OSSL_provider_init
+
+And it should offer no algorithms by default, exactly six with
+`enable-legacy-allowed`, and eight with DES enabled as well:
+
+    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$PWD OPENSSL_CONF=/dev/null
+    ./apps/openssl list -digest-algorithms -cipher-algorithms \
+        -provider-path $PWD/providers -provider legacy | grep '@ legacy'
+    ./apps/openssl list -digest-algorithms -cipher-algorithms \
+        -provider-path $PWD/providers -provider legacy \
+        -provparam legacy:enable-legacy-allowed=1 | grep '@ legacy'
+    ./apps/openssl list -digest-algorithms -cipher-algorithms \
+        -provider-path $PWD/providers -provider legacy \
+        -provparam legacy:enable-legacy-allowed=1 \
+        -provparam legacy:enable-des=1 | grep '@ legacy'
+
+The first command prints nothing, yet `openssl list -providers` with the same
+`-provider-path` and `-provider` options still lists the legacy provider as
+active.  It is still loaded and configured under the name `legacy`, but
+reports its own name as follows:
+
+    $ ./apps/openssl list -providers -provider-path $PWD/providers -provider legacy
+    Providers:
+      legacy
+        name: Chainguard Legacy Allowed FIPS 140-3 I.G. 2.4.A Provider
+        version: 4.0.3
+        status: active
+
+Known-answer checks, forcing every fetch through this provider:
+
+    P="-provider-path $PWD/providers -provider legacy -propquery provider=legacy"
+    P="$P -provparam legacy:enable-legacy-allowed=1"
+    PD="$P -provparam legacy:enable-des=1"
+    printf 'abc' > /tmp/abc
+
+    ./apps/openssl dgst -md5  $P -r /tmp/abc   # 900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72
+    ./apps/openssl dgst -sha1 $P -r /tmp/abc   # a9993e364706816aba3e25717850c26c9cd0d89d
+    ./apps/openssl dgst -md4  $P -r /tmp/abc   # a448017aaf21d8525fc10ae87aa6729d
+    ./apps/openssl dgst -sha224 $P -r /tmp/abc # 23097d223405d8228642a477bda255b32aadbce4bda0b3f7e36c9da7
+
+AES-ECB against FIPS-197 C.1 and C.3:
+
+    printf '\x00\x11\x22\x33\x44\x55\x66\x77\x88\x99\xaa\xbb\xcc\xdd\xee\xff' > /tmp/pt
+    ./apps/openssl enc -aes-128-ecb $P -nopad -in /tmp/pt \
+        -K 000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f | xxd -p
+    # 69c4e0d86a7b0430d8cdb78070b4c55a
+    ./apps/openssl enc -aes-256-ecb $P -nopad -in /tmp/pt \
+        -K 000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f101112131415161718191a1b1c1d1e1f | xxd -p
+    # 8ea2b7ca516745bfeafc49904b496089
+
+DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC against the vectors in
+`test/recipes/30-test_evp_data/evpciph_des.txt` and `evpciph_des3_common.txt`:
+
+    printf '\x4e\x6f\x77\x20\x69\x73\x20\x74' > /tmp/pt8
+    ./apps/openssl enc -des-cbc $PD -nopad -in /tmp/pt8 \
+        -K 0123456789abcdef -iv 1234567890abcdef | xxd -p
+    # e5c7cdde872bf27c
+
+    printf '7654321 Now is the time for \x00\x00\x00\x00' > /tmp/pt32
+    ./apps/openssl enc -des-ede3-cbc $PD -nopad -in /tmp/pt32 \
+        -K 0123456789abcdeff1e0d3c2b5a49786fedcba9876543210 \
+        -iv fedcba9876543210 | xxd -p
+    # 3fe301c962ac01d02213763c1cbd4cdc799657c064ecf5d41c673812cfde9675
+
+Testing
+-------
+
+`make test` (and its alias `make tests`) cannot be used: it depends on
+`build_programs_nodep`, which builds every test program and therefore hits the
+two link failures described under "Limitations" below.
+
+Use `make run_tests` instead.  It is a separate target with no build
+dependency, so it runs the harness against whatever is already built:
+
+    make -j4 build_generated
+    make -j4 providers/legacy.so libssl.so
+    make -j4 apps/openssl test/evp_test test/provider_test
+    make -j4 build_modules_nodep util/wrap.pl link-utils
+
+    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$PWD
+    make run_tests TESTS='test_legacy_okay test_provider'
+
+`LD_LIBRARY_PATH` has to be exported: an in-tree build gives its binaries no
+`RUNPATH`, and `util/wrap.pl` does not set a library path either, so without it
+the recipes fail with
+
+    ../../apps/openssl: error while loading shared libraries: libssl.so.4:
+    cannot open shared object file: No such file or directory
+
+`util/wrap.pl` and `link-utils` are needed for the same reason -- the harness
+invokes every program through the wrapper, and a partial build does not create
+it.
+
+`TESTS` accepts recipe names without their numeric prefix, shell globs
+(`TESTS='test_evp*'`), a numeric prefix on its own (`TESTS=30` runs
+`30-*.t`), and exclusions (`TESTS='-test_ssl*'`).  `make list-tests` prints
+every available name.
+
+### Exercising the algorithms directly
+
+The EVP data files that cover this provider's algorithms can be run one at
+a time.  These three pass in full:
+
+    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$PWD OPENSSL_MODULES=$PWD/providers
+    D=test/recipes/30-test_evp_data
+    C=test/default-and-legacy.cnf
+
+    ./test/evp_test -config $C $D/evpmd_md.txt             # MD4, MD5
+    ./test/evp_test -config $C $D/evpmd_sha.txt            # SHA1, SHA2-224
+    ./test/evp_test -config $C $D/evpciph_aes_common.txt   # AES-ECB
+
+No stock data file exercises the two DES ciphers in isolation.
+`evp_test` stops a file at the first cipher it cannot fetch, and both
+`evpciph_des.txt` (DES-ECB, line 9) and `evpciph_des3_common.txt`
+(DES-EDE3-ECB, after its CBC stanzas) hit one that this module does not
+offer; the latter also fetches DES-EDE3-CBC from the default provider when
+both are loaded.  Use the `openssl enc` known-answer checks under
+"Verifying" above, which force the fetch to `provider=legacy` and pass
+`enable-des=1`.  (`test/legacy.cnf` and `test/default-and-legacy.cnf` set
+`enable-legacy-allowed` but not `enable-des`, so DES stanzas would be
+unavailable under them anyway.)
+
+**`OPENSSL_MODULES` must be set for these.**  Without it, `evp_test` cannot
+find the module, silently skips every legacy stanza with
+
+    # skipping, 'legacy' provider not available: .../evpciph_des.txt:11
+
+and still exits 0 — so a broken provider looks like a pass.  `make run_tests`
+sets it automatically (`test/run_tests.pl`), but a direct invocation does not.
+Note also that `evpmd_md.txt` skips its MD2 stanzas because MD2 is disabled in
+a default build; MD4 and MD5 are genuinely exercised.
+
+### Tests that fail by design
+
+Reducing the algorithm set breaks the recipes that exercise what was removed.
+`TESTS=test_evp` fails as a whole; the individual data files behave as
+follows:
+
+| Data file | Result | Cause |
+|-----------|--------|-------|
+| `evpciph_des.txt` | fails | stops at DES-ECB on line 9; DES-CFB*, DES-OFB, DES-EDE3-CFB*, DES-EDE3-OFB and DESX-CBC are also absent, so only the DES-CBC stanzas could pass |
+| `evpciph_bf.txt`, `evpciph_cast5.txt`, `evpciph_idea.txt`, `evpciph_rc2.txt`, `evpciph_rc4.txt`, `evpciph_seed.txt` | fail | ciphers removed |
+| `evpmd_mdc2.txt`, `evpmd_whirlpool.txt` | fail | digests removed |
+| `evpkdf_pbkdf1.txt` | fails | PBKDF1 removed; note this file is in `30-test_evp.t`'s default list, not its legacy-only list |
+
+`evpciph_rc5.txt` and `evpciph_rc4_stitched.txt` still pass only because RC5
+and the stitched RC4-HMAC-MD5 cipher are absent from a default build anyway.
+
+How it works
+------------
+
+Everything compiled into `liblegacy.a` is built with `LEGACY_MODULE` defined
+(`DEFINE[$LIBLEGACY]` in `providers/build.info`).  The same sources are still
+compiled without it for `libcrypto`, `libdefault.a` and `libfips.a`, so only the
+legacy copies are affected.  Two code paths are compiled out under that macro
+because they would otherwise pull in most of the library:
+
+* `OSSL_PARAM_get_BN()` / `OSSL_PARAM_set_BN()` in `crypto/params.c` are the
+  only users of `crypto/bn` here.  Removing them removes BIGNUM support from
+  the module entirely.  No algorithm listed above uses BIGNUM parameters.
+
+* `ossl_cipher_tlsunpadblock()` in
+  `providers/implementations/ciphers/ciphercommon_block.c`, and its call site in
+  `ciphercommon.c`, reach `ssl/record/methods/tls_pad.c`, which calls
+  `RAND_bytes_ex()` on its constant-time failure path.  No TLS-capable cipher is
+  registered, so the path is unreachable; under `LEGACY_MODULE` the call site
+  fails loudly instead.
+
+* The random-key generators behind `OSSL_CIPHER_PARAM_RANDOM_KEY` in
+  `cipher_des.c` and `cipher_tdes_common.c`, reached through
+  `EVP_CIPHER_CTX_rand_key()`, normally call `RAND_priv_bytes_ex()`, which
+  would pull in the DRBG.  They now go through
+  `ossl_cipher_generic_rand_bytes()` in `ciphercommon.c`, which under
+  `LEGACY_MODULE` instead asks the core for entropy with the
+  `OSSL_FUNC_get_user_entropy` / `OSSL_FUNC_get_entropy` upcalls — the same
+  path the FIPS module uses to seed its DRBGs.  `legacyprov.c` records those
+  callbacks at init with `ossl_prov_seeding_from_dispatch()` (so
+  `providers/common/provider_seeding.c` is now compiled into `liblegacy.a`
+  too), and the cipher context carries the provider context so the call can
+  be made.  The bytes
+  come from the parent `libcrypto`'s seed source (the OS random device via
+  `getrandom(2)` on Linux) rather than its DRBG; the parity-fixing step is
+  unchanged.  Encryption and decryption never touch this path — IVs are
+  supplied by the caller at the EVP layer, and nothing else in the module
+  needs randomness.
+
+`legacyprov.c` also no longer creates a child `OSSL_LIB_CTX`.  All eight
+algorithms are self-contained and never fetch anything from the core, and not
+creating one is what removes the last dependency on `libcrypto`.  The two
+remaining upcalls — entropy for random DES keys, and `core_get_params` for
+`enable-legacy-allowed` and `enable-des` — go through the `OSSL_CORE_HANDLE`
+the core passes at init, not through a library context.  Both parameters are
+evaluated in the provider's `query_operation` callback with
+`ossl_prov_ctx_get_bool_param()`: with `enable-legacy-allowed` off the
+callback returns `NULL` for every operation, and the DES entries sit at the
+front of the cipher table so the DES-disabled view is the same array starting
+two entries in.
+
+The implementations themselves are added to `liblegacy.a` by the usual
+mechanism — a `SOURCE[../../providers/liblegacy.a]` line guarded on
+`!$disabled{module} && !$disabled{shared}`, mirroring what `crypto/md5` and
+`crypto/md4` already did — in `crypto/sha`, `crypto/aes`, `crypto/modes`,
+`crypto/des` and `crypto/buffer`, plus a short list of utility files in
+`crypto/build.info`.  `crypto/des` previously fed `liblegacy.a` only when
+`no-deprecated` hid the DES functions from `libcrypto`'s export list; a module
+that does not link `libcrypto` needs them unconditionally.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+* **`make` does not complete.**  `test/endecode_test` and
+  `test/evp_extra_test` link `providers/liblegacy.a` together with
+  `libcrypto.a`, and now hit multiple-definition errors on the sources that
+  `liblegacy.a` carries its own copies of (`params.o`, `mem.o`, `o_str.o` and
+  so on).  Build `providers/legacy.so` as its own target, as shown above.
+  Making those two test programs link again is unfinished work; they would
+  need to stop linking `liblegacy.a` directly.
+
+* This is not a supported OpenSSL configuration.  Providers other than FIPS are
+  not designed to be freestanding, and the algorithms offered here are
+  obsolete: MD4, MD5 and SHA1 are all broken for security purposes,
+  single DES has a 56-bit key, Triple DES is disallowed by NIST for
+  encryption since 2024 and both DES ciphers share the 64-bit block size
+  that makes them vulnerable to Sweet32, and AES-ECB is not a general-purpose
+  mode.  This module exists so that the
+  formats and protocols listed under "FIPS properties" above can still be
+  parsed, not to protect anything.
